  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe central idea is to improve the given n=70 solution by using a combination of deterministic optimization and adaptive sampling in the space of piecewise linear functions. This method leverages the observation that optimal functions often exhibit self-similarity or periodic structure, while avoiding the computational burden of Bayesian optimization by targeting critical regions of the solution space. \n\nThe key limitation addressed is the non-convex nature of the problem and the expensive evaluation cost. The approach differs from previous strategies by:\n- Using deterministic bounds generation from the initial solution\n- Implementing an adaptive landscape exploration\n- Employing direct search within transformed spaces\n- Maintaining exact convexity conditions through dual formulation projections\n\nBottlenecks addressed:\n- Expensive objective evaluations via correlation\n- Need for parallel computation for better performance\n- Avoiding the curse of dimensionality through structure exploitation\n\n2. Mathematical specification\na. Representation\n- h is represented as a piecewise linear function with fixed kinks obtained by scaling and integer constraints\n- Vectorization reduces dimensions by enforcing symmetry conditions\n\nb. Key transformations\n- Change of variables: yield coordinates (a\u1d62) \u2208 \u211d\u207f\u208a \n- Constraint mapping: sum(a\u1d62) = n/2\n- Objective becomes max\u2096 \u222bg(\u03bbk) d\u03bb (simplified integral)\n\nc. Optimization formulation\n- Minimize C5(h)\n- Subject to: 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1, \u222bh(x)dx = 1\n- With the objective closely related to the maximum correlation\n\nd. Algorithm selection\n- Uses DC (difference of convex functions) programming\n- Implements projected gradient with adaptive step sizes\n- Includes shadow simplex projection for convex relaxations\n- Complies with CPLEX branching for mixed-integer demands\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na. Initialization\n- n_points = 100 (unprocessable: set failure message)\n- Convert initial_h_values to polar coordinate form\n- Generate periodic sampling patterns (phase rotation)\n\nb. Core optimization loop\n- Phase 1: Preprocess samples to intepolate piecewise\n- Phase 2: Solve projected gradient steps\n- Phase 3: Update bounds using projected shadow methods\n  while time < budget_s:\n    - Sample new directions in alignment spaces\n    - Clip gradients to avoid boundary distruption\n    - Conduct dual feasibility checks\n\nc. Repair mechanism\n- Continuity enforcement: recover piecewise structure\n- Projection: approximate via integer set constraints\n- Maintain symmetry between analyzed pauses and boundaries\n\nd. Termination\n- Minimum C5 improvement threshold\n- Exhausted time budget\n- Achievement of boundary satisfaction tolerance\n\n4. Search choices\n- Initial n_points = 100\n- Vector transformation for optimization\n- Refinement in 7 amidst 20 seeds\n- Shadow zone width adaptive to problem granularity\n\n5. Budget and robustness\n- Dominant cost: Matrix operations of size n\u00d7n\n- Compute profiles: Update shadow variables require \n- Recovery fallback: Generic SLSQP with repair\n- Objectively superiority evaluation: Fit to [-] and compare to lower bounds\n\n6. Validation\n- Check finite values for h and 1-h\n- Ensure sum(h) = n_points/2\n- Compute correlation via np.correlate\n- Projected validation ensures alignment with objective function",
